From: Godshatter <mentat@telerama.lm.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Dec 1994 14:07:34 +0500 (EST)
Subject: [#94-12-221] singularity

On Sun, 4 Dec 1994, Robin Hanson wrote:

> Perry wrote:
> > ... replace our neurons with nanomachines, ... The individual
> > who does this will suddenly find that for every day that passes to
> > other people, 27 THOUSAND years have passed in subjective time to him.
> 
> Jordan Sparks writes:
> >This is a bunch of garbage.  
> 
> Last time Perry reposted this, I disagreed strongly.  Glad to see this
> time there is a chorus of disagreement.  For those who defend Perry's
> position, it should be clear that just reposting Perry's post is not a
> sufficient argument -- you must explain to us in more detail just why
> you expect such a sudden transition, as opposed to continuing
> accelerating change.
> 
> Robin Hanson

It wasn't intended to be a sufficient argument.  I reposted it because 
someone was inquiring as to what was being referred to by "singularity", 
if memory serves.  Besides, since we are talking about something that is 
projected to happen sometime in the future, and as that post pointed out, 
somewhat ambiguous of definition, it cannot be proven in a rigorous way.

As to a continuing acceleration versus sudden transition, "suddenness" 
depends on point of view.  To a being thinking a million times slower 
than ourselves - such as one of those briefly described in Clarke's 
"Childhood's End", our transition from spear to computer would probably 
appear sudden.  The same applies vis a vis human and something that 
thinks a million times faster.  So it would not appear sudden to those 
experiencing the singularity from the inside.  But from our current 
perspective, it would appear sudden.

