Its a really nice example of how memes compete or use each other. The Net 
really makes memetics more obvious. 

Da'Claw wrote:

>Does anyone believe it possible to insert
>mind-viruses into subjects without them being aware of the process?

This happens all the time. You cannot learn anything without getting some 
memes in the bargain. But most are quickly forgotten or out-competed.


>        By this I mean that by some feat of verbal programming, the
>thought-virus will insert itself into the human processor/brain, unravel and
>then propagate itself.

Not directly. Human minds are quite varied, and its very hard to unravel 
them using only a meme (it can be done, but it isn't 100% effective and 
takes a lot of time). To function, it would have to take over the 
conscious level, which is quite hard since its distributed. Had it been 
centralized and logical like in a computer, then it would have been possible.


>        Is this all brainwashing by another name?

Brainwashing works by having the washer totally in control over the 
environment and life of the victim, who is completely helpless. The 
helplessness makes the victim regress to a more childish state, and the 
brainwasher can step in as a surrogate parent (this is essentially the 
Stockholm Effect but consciously applied). Memes don't brainwash people, 
but they can make people brainwash each other. And themselves.


